#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11d3f9e1-dad0-51d4-86a9-e0c49465cc3c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a48ca0e-93ed-5174-bab6-4c899fe31648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241997} {:answer/id #uuid "81c43c9e-66cb-55e7-98de-48e2ff68d8cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242317} {:answer/id #uuid "822c3964-a365-5ee7-be32-2b3f5876cb91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242321} {:answer/id #uuid "a81cda8b-cfe4-5e44-8bea-ed48a8910f53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242656} {:answer/id #uuid "c3bb5dcb-f2aa-5a8d-a14d-49c9a860d41c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3bb5dcb-f2aa-5a8d-a14d-49c9a860d41c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242899} :answer-field/id #uuid "14ae7574-9b24-5483-829f-b98eb44bc08b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4e3c164e-33ad-5643-8072-cdedeec68994" :question/math-academy-id "q-109147" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(2,2)$ and $(-2,2),$ traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7ca4b6fe3a5a16c4004c75b0e4524cc3b42550cb2413d5b8e1c21a1d53cee902.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-y\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{[{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133884 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48652211-e469-55b9-96cb-2526a4135d34" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral as a Repeated Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c6fb1e6-13c2-5304-9a0b-fd9f38873188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242152} {:answer/id #uuid "8855dd4e-cf52-5421-a738-17951cb4daa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{x+2}^{{x}^{2}+x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242377} {:answer/id #uuid "c441ba1b-5791-5a9d-88bf-8622cc73ba19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242905} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6ea074-1c33-5438-b183-744a1d23db35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242975} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d53892-c143-515e-b312-92905c82dc37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}(2y-x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c6fb1e6-13c2-5304-9a0b-fd9f38873188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242152} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a6164af-b887-57b9-91dd-6c4ddd92fdb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a5b7043e-fa21-5314-98d6-598e5c27911d" :question/math-academy-id "q-109263" :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=x+2.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}⟩,$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/e3/e32b8e7d6ce80d7cedad928f9a2f7acc3c3d7f06add7909e856db27d154068d6.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133884 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48652211-e469-55b9-96cb-2526a4135d34" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral as a Repeated Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "003753be-91cd-5401-b185-1a2caa7b0ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}x\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323978} {:answer/id #uuid "11403fb2-cc40-5faf-bc1a-820a3836d9e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(x+y)dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324129} {:answer/id #uuid "14390247-b8d4-5256-ae63-0dceb79e5d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324154} {:answer/id #uuid "acbb3d66-c472-5d9a-9faa-95357fdcbd7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}(x-y)dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325437} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e9f50f-53fb-5ee2-baa4-64890b3e25c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6e9f50f-53fb-5ee2-baa4-64890b3e25c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326148} :answer-field/id #uuid "c166fb2a-6179-53b7-8884-9c52889f51d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dfeb76a7-b3b4-5da5-8979-e2b8f3b5d4af" :question/math-academy-id "q-41702" :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the $x$-axis, for $\\pi \\le x\\le 2\\pi .$ Express ${\\int }_{C}xdx-(x-y)dy$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/264cb888010525848f647669653d34383535dd32e6ac264927105540c3b85f9f.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=-(x-y).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):\\pi \\le x\\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the Green's Theorem we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xdx-(x-y)dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11d3f9e1-dad0-51d4-86a9-e0c49465cc3c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7924d408-b0a6-57f4-9535-29236f3d016b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415810} {:answer/id #uuid "950ad376-e54c-57af-bfa1-45e819b52db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi ({\\pi }^{2}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415816} {:answer/id #uuid "ab82bc31-acab-5197-b9ab-1bdf4558c405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi ({\\pi }^{2}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415820} {:answer/id #uuid "c5551b46-21d7-59b0-b72c-4198c2a76d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415830} {:answer/id #uuid "d84a9c83-a85a-5496-be1f-846504032a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "950ad376-e54c-57af-bfa1-45e819b52db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi ({\\pi }^{2}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415816} :answer-field/id #uuid "b60e1b91-b85b-56e9-90f2-353a69264f24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4381a978-8315-576b-b33a-7ce6b2ca6e4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-41810" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,{x}^{2}-2y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the square $0\\le x\\le \\pi ,0\\le y\\le \\pi ,$ traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/4f/4f75ee554c61dc799273c2c731d608766d27987937d0058df6ee1e0290d8b060.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+({x}^{2}-2y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}-2y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\pi ,0\\le y\\le \\pi \\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+({x}^{2}-2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{[2xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]}_{0}^{\\pi }\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\pi x+2)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\pi {x}^{2}+2x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{3}+2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi ({\\pi }^{2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11d3f9e1-dad0-51d4-86a9-e0c49465cc3c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c5474dd-5be4-50d8-bebe-7eb5eb66bf7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324460} {:answer/id #uuid "44ecd8ea-bdce-5d1f-a1a0-626d659605b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324527} {:answer/id #uuid "d8cd2f91-5b3e-58ed-b480-0e9af6226381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325857} {:answer/id #uuid "de55e393-f59f-5979-bf5b-51218edda1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325912} {:answer/id #uuid "f5723cad-795b-55bb-8ab0-563ed1f0d4a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c5474dd-5be4-50d8-bebe-7eb5eb66bf7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324460} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c9e60ff-5c2c-5bd5-a03b-c43d94b21994" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "63bc1464-04bd-5198-8418-9e8745d1335c" :question/math-academy-id "q-41811" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{3}+2{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(2,0)$ and $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/73/73bf9b973e50c540a873f4f1a44d936474e4c49d7041a330902508a840645009.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{3}+2{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):y\\le x\\le 2-y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}-4xy]}_{y}^{2-y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-y)^{2}-4(2-y)y-{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{2}-12y+4)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-3y+1)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{3}{2}+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
